<body class="how-page">
<SvgDefs />
<Nav variant="how" />

<!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
<header class="how-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-eyebrow">Benchmark method</div>
    <h1>We threw our first benchmark away. Here's the one that replaced it.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Every tool in this category publishes a percentage. Almost none of them publish the questions,
      the answers, or the runs those percentages came from, which makes the percentage
      unfalsifiable and therefore worthless. This page is the method behind ours: where the
      questions come from, how the right answer is decided, what we throw out, and the measurement
      problem that invalidated our own first set of numbers. It works on any skill, rules file, or
      tool, not just this one.
    </p>
    <a class="how-back" href="/">← back to the overview</a>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ===================== 1. WHAT IS MEASURED ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>What gets measured: finding the files, not finishing the job</h2>
      <p>
        An agent's session splits into two phases. First it works out <i>which files matter</i>.
        Then it reads, reasons and edits. Only the first phase is what a navigation or memory tool
        changes. The second is the same work in both arms, so including it adds cost to both sides
        equally and dilutes the thing being measured.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">The task</span>
        <span class="fv">Every prompt is an exploration question. The deliverable is a list of file
        paths, never a code change, and the output contract says so.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Ground truth</span>
        <span class="fv">The source files touched by the pull request that closed the issue, filtered
        to extensions the tool can parse. Mechanical, so scoring is reproducible rather than
        hand-graded.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Reported</span>
        <span class="fv">Whole-session token usage and turn count for the arm, alongside how many
        gold files it actually named. Cost without a correctness number rewards a tool for giving
        up early.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="how-aside">
      <b>Why whole-session, not per-call.</b> Roughly 90% of what a session costs is cached context
      replayed on every turn, so a tool that trims one tool result while adding a turn can make its
      own output smaller and your bill larger. The only honest denominator is the whole session.
      <a href="/blog/why-most-token-savings-tools-lie/">The long version is here.</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 2. WHERE THE QUESTIONS COME FROM ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>The questions have to be written by someone who didn't know the answer</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part that decides whether a benchmark measures anything. If the prompt names the
        file, both arms find it instantly and the experiment is over before it starts. Commit
        messages and PR descriptions are written <i>afterwards, by the person who did the work</i>,
        so they name classes and paths as a matter of course. We tried mining them and threw the
        approach out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Issue bodies have the opposite property. They are written by someone describing a symptom
        before anyone knows where the cause lives, so they carry the domain vocabulary without the
        map. That structural fact, not careful editing, is what keeps the prompts honest.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">1 · select</span>
        <span class="fv">Closed issues with a linked merged PR. The PR must touch at least 2 and at
        most 8 source files: one-file fixes reward grep rather than navigation, and mass refactors
        make the overlap score meaningless.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">2 · scrub</span>
        <span class="fv">Strip file paths, stack frames, code blocks, image embeds and issue
        references from the body. What survives should still say what is broken, and not where.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">3 · reshape</span>
        <span class="fv">Rewrite the report as an exploration question, preserving the author's own
        vocabulary and introducing no new terms. Both the original and the rewrite are saved so the
        pass is auditable.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">4 · drop</span>
        <span class="fv">Discard anything too vague to navigate from, or that lost too much of its
        body to scrubbing, which is the signature of an issue that was over-specific to begin
        with.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="sec-tail">
      Roughly a third of candidates do not survive. The dropped ones are published with their
      reasons, because an exclusion log you can read is the only evidence that the surviving set
      wasn't chosen to flatter the result.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 3. THE BIAS FINDING ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>The problem that invalidated our first benchmark</h2>
      <p>
        Our first set of numbers came from Kafka, Django and Mastodon. Reasonable choices: large,
        real, well-structured. They are also three of the most-discussed codebases on the internet,
        which turns out to matter more than any of their engineering properties.
      </p>
      <p>
        The test is simple and anyone can run it. Take the baseline arm, the one with no tool
        installed, and look at its <i>first</i> move, before it has read a single file. Extract the
        internal class names it used. Subtract anything the prompt itself supplied. Whatever is left
        did not come from the codebase, because the agent hadn't looked at the codebase yet. It came
        from the model's memory of that project.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Kafka</span>
        <span class="fv"><b>21 of 25 sessions (84%)</b> named internal class names before opening a
        file.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Django</span>
        <span class="fv"><b>7 of 14 (50%)</b>.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Mastodon</span>
        <span class="fv"><b>6 of 18 (33%)</b>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This does not merely add noise. It bends the result in a specific direction. Prior knowledge
      makes the <i>baseline</i> cheaper, because an agent that already knows where to look skips the
      exploration it would otherwise pay for. So the better the model knows your benchmark repo, the
      smaller any tool's improvement appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our own savings ranked Mastodon 43%, Django 28%, Kafka 15%, which is the exact inverse of the
      leak rates above. The ranking we had been reading as a fact about languages and codebase
      structure was substantially a fact about what the model had already read.
    </p>

    <div class="how-aside">
      <b>What we did about it.</b> Renaming the repos to defeat recognition needs AST-consistent
      rewrites across declarations, imports, build config and tests, and a surface-level rename
      corrupts the baseline instead of cleaning it. So we retired those three repos and rebuilt the
      benchmark on four less-discussed codebases. The old numbers are not on this site because we no
      longer believe them.
    </div>

    <p class="sec-tail">
      If you are reading someone else's benchmark, this is the first question to ask, and it applies
      to every tool in this category including ours. A result measured on a famous repository is
      measuring the model's memory as much as the tool.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 4. THE NUMBERS ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>What it says about coldstart</h2>
      <p>
        Two repositories, JMRI (Java) and Arches (Python), run under the method above. These are not
        large improvements, and publishing them next to the method that produced them is the point.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TODO(numbers): fill from the run summaries before publishing. Do not
         estimate these. Each row needs the arm definitions, n, and the run count. -->
    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row"><span class="fk">JMRI · Java</span><span class="fv">__ prompts, __ runs per arm. Exploration cost __%, gold-file recall __%.</span></div>
      <div class="flag-row"><span class="fk">Arches · Python</span><span class="fv">__ prompts, __ runs per arm. Exploration cost __%, gold-file recall __%.</span></div>
    </div>

    <p class="sec-tail">
      Both sets of prompts, their gold files, and the dropped candidates with reasons are published
      alongside the tooling, so the run is repeatable against our own artifacts.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 5. RUN IT YOURSELF ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>Run it on something that isn't ours</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing here is specific to coldstart. The same setup measures a skill, a rules file, an MCP
        server, or a change to your own prompt. Two copies of one repository, the thing under test
        installed in exactly one of them, the same questions asked in both, one session per question
        so that a later prompt never rides on an earlier one's context.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Measuring</span>
        <span class="fv"><a href="https://github.com/AkashGoenka/convotokens">convotokens</a> reads
        your own local transcripts and reports whole-session tokens and turns, including subagents.
        No network calls, no dollar estimates.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">One prompt, one session</span>
        <span class="fv">Otherwise the third question is cheap because the first two already loaded
        the context, and you are measuring conversation order.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Same model, both arms</span>
        <span class="fv">Including subagents. A mixed-model arm compared against a single-model arm
        is measuring the models.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flag-row">
        <span class="fk">Repeat before believing</span>
        <span class="fv">Agent runs vary. Run one arm several times unchanged and look at the spread
        before treating any difference between arms as real.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="how-aside">
      <b>A private codebase is the best substrate available.</b> The bias above cannot exist in a
      repository the model has never seen. If you are evaluating a tool for your team, running it on
      your own closed code gives you a cleaner experiment than any public benchmark can.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 6. WHAT WE DON'T CLAIM ===================== -->
<section class="how-sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="how-sec-head">
      <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flags">
      <div class="flag-row"><span class="fk">Not task completion</span><span class="fv">It measures locating the right files. Whether the eventual patch was good is a different experiment.</span></div>
      <div class="flag-row"><span class="fk">Not your codebase</span><span class="fv">Two repositories in two languages. Your layout, conventions and history will move the number in either direction.</span></div>
      <div class="flag-row"><span class="fk">Not a dollar figure</span><span class="fv">Token counts are usage, not billing. Subscription pricing makes any dollar conversion an invention.</span></div>
    </div>

    <p class="sec-tail">
      The two essays this method came out of go further into how a benchmark misleads its own author:
      <a href="/blog/why-most-token-savings-tools-lie/">why most token-savings numbers lie</a> and
      <a href="/blog/how-your-own-benchmark-lies-to-you-first/">how your own benchmark lies to you first</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

